Choosing where to open a business, or judging whether a neighbourhood is safe, well connected and affordable, normally means days or weeks of manual surveys across maps, property listings and reports. Geographic Information Systems can answer such questions, but they demand skill in spatial data and query languages. The large language models (LLMs) that could make them accessible tend to invent place names and coordinates when they answer directly, which makes their output impossible to verify.

This project presents a natural-language geospatial query and decision-support system built on a spatial database. A user types a question in plain English, such as "hospitals within 1 km of a metro station". An LLM pipeline first checks that the question is a clear spatial query and then writes a PostGIS query against a documented description of the database schema. The query is validated and executed under a read-only database role, and if it fails or returns nothing, the model is asked, within fixed limits, for a corrected query. The rows are returned as a GeoJSON layer drawn on an interactive MapLibre map, together with a short explanation and the exact SQL that was run, so that every answer can be checked.

The system runs on PostgreSQL 16 with PostGIS 3.4, serves vector tiles with Martin, and has a FastAPI backend and a React frontend. A loading pipeline built on OpenStreetMap and OSMnx fills the database for any city polygon. The deployed instance holds Mumbai, with 48 administrative boundaries, 16,800 road segments, 2,200 transit stops, 10,800 businesses and points of interest, a 76,000-row house-price dataset and area-level safety scores for 40 neighbourhoods. Four decision tools use the same data: a site report for a clicked location, a competition and catchment analysis that suggests lower-competition spots, a cost-to-open and break-even estimator, and a side-by-side comparison of up to three locations. The tools label measured values (counts, distances and median prices) separately from modelled estimates (rent, revenue and payback).

A benchmark of 39 spatial questions with hand-written gold queries shows that the engine returns exactly the gold answer for 74.4% of the questions (geometry F1 88.4%) and that every generated query ran. Most of the failures come from a row cap and the strictness of exact-match scoring. A direct-LLM baseline that names places and coordinates without a database returned names that exist in the database only 19.5% of the time. A sensitivity study of the earlier weighted-sum ranking showed that it is stable under small changes to one weight but not under a change of weighting scheme, which is why the new tools report measured quantities. The limitations are stated openly: a small single-run benchmark, no real rental data, heuristic demand and revenue models, and listing locations that are accurate only to the region. The work shows that having an LLM write a query that is executed on real data gives answers that are both useful and verifiable.

*Keywords:* natural-language interface, geospatial query, text-to-SQL, PostGIS, large language models, site selection
